---
title: 'Invariants: a compiler for better LLM structured outputs'
description: >-
  An inference-time verification engine designed to enforce deterministic,
  context-sensitive constraints during autoregressive LLM generation.
date: 2026-08-18
href: https://github.com/ujaandas/invariants
---
For the uninitiated, structured outputs/decoding refers to how LLMs "enforce" a schema that they have been told to adhere to - an OpenAPI spec, a JSON schema, etc...

Existing structured decoding libraries (like Outlines or Guidance) rely on Context-Free Grammars (CFGs) and Finite State Machines (FSMs). While they guarantee surface syntax - like matching JSON braces or enforcing field types - they are fundamentally incapable of validating **Context-Sensitive Invariants (CSIs)**.

A CFG cannot enforce cross-field logic, like verifying that an assigned IP address falls within a declared subnet or ensuring that `total_price == unit_price * quantity`. When these rules break in agentic workflows or infrastructure provisioning, systems have to fall back on high-latency **post-hoc rejection sampling**: waiting for the full payload to generate, catching the runtime failure downstream, and prompting a costly retry.

### How `invariants` solves that problem

**invariants** moves formal semantic verification directly into the token generation loop in C++. By validating partial generation state against schema invariants at each step, illegal tokens are pruned before they are ever sampled.

- **Custom DSL:** A schema definition language for declaring cross-field rules, dynamic ranges, and operational invariants.
- **Compiler Pipeline:** Uses multi-stage tree walks and targeted codegen passes to turn schema constraints into fast evaluation routines for the sampler.
- **Runtime Integration:** Evaluates constraints during the forward pass with minimal per-token overhead.

> *(Note: While a custom bytecode VM was part of the original design, the current multi-pass tree walk and codegen architecture handles evaluation cleanly. A dedicated VM remains on the roadmap if performance becomes a bottleneck).*
